course: everyday
lesson: 434
title: The friend with a PS6
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Ryan in Leeds told the class he had a PS6 — when no PS6 was on sale anywhere. «My dad works for Sony, innit, it's a secret test one.» A girl looked it up and announced there was no PS6. Ryan said his was «special». Nobody believed a word he said for the rest of term. Boasting lies are told to look cool, and they usually cost the very thing they were meant to win: people's respect.
explain: Boasts often have a tell: the thing can never be seen. Tom's friend had «like five hundred» rare Pokémon cards — «in a safe, at the bank». Mason had an iPhone 18 in his bag that he never took out, «because Mum says no phones at school», but he had «just texted» someone at break. Zara in Johannesburg met a famous footballer at the airport and showed a blurry photo of a tall man's back. Fuzzy proof is not proof — but you can enjoy a story without needing to decide it is true.
explain: Some boasts crash into facts. Oliver «got to level 100 in one weekend» in a game that takes weeks; he said he was just goated — slang for «the greatest of all time» — then that his mum «deleted» his account. Josh said he could do a backflip; at the park it was his ankle, then his back, then wet grass. In the end his mates said «just show us a cartwheel». He could. They cheered, and he felt better than after all the excuses.
explain: Kindness can save a friendship when a boast comes out. Chloe said her dad was a millionaire; when Mia came round to a normal flat with a cosy sofa, Chloe went red. Mia just said «Your flat's lush, I love your sofa» («lush» is Welsh and West Country slang for lovely), and Chloe stopped the millionaire story. They stayed friends. People often lie because they feel small. Lucy said she'd won a dance competition; a friend's mum had been there: «You came fifth, sweetheart — and it was brilliant.» The class clapped for fifth anyway.
explain: Not every lie is about showing off. Ava told her best friend she had «a dentist appointment» instead of saying she'd chosen another party. Photos from that party went on the group chat, and her friend cried; the lie hurt more than the truth would have. Jaylen in Chicago said his big brother was «in the NBA, on a secret team». His brother, home from college, laughed: «Bro, I'm on the chess team.» Jaylen learned that the real thing was pretty cool too.
fact: Boasting lies are told to look cool or to fit in, and when found out they cost people's trust.
fact: You can check some boasts with facts: whether a product exists, how long a game level takes.
fact: If someone always has a reason why you can't see the thing, it is probably a story, not a fact.
fact: A blurry or faraway photo is not real proof.
fact: You can enjoy a story without deciding whether it is true.
fact: People often lie because they feel small; being kind when the lie comes out can save the friendship.
fact: A good friend lets you climb down from a boast; «I can't actually, but I can do this» is fine.
fact: Kind honesty, like «I'm going to another party, sorry I can't do both», hurts less than a lie found out later.
fact: A real result, like fifth place, is worth more than a fake first.
fact: What is really true about people you love is enough; bigging it up makes the real thing look small.
fact: Not every lie needs to be caught; sometimes the kind thing is to let it drop and like the person anyway.
fact: «Goated» means the greatest of all time; «lush» (Wales, West Country) means lovely; «innit» (London) means «isn't it» or «right?».
exercise: What did Ryan say about his PS6? | It was a secret test one from his dad at Sony | He won it in a big online gaming competition last month | He bought it with birthday money | His uncle built it himself from old parts
exercise: How did the class find out the PS6 story was false? | A girl looked it up — there was no PS6 | Ryan's dad came to school and said so himself | Ryan brought in a box with no console inside | Sony wrote a letter to the school about it
exercise: What did Mia say when she saw Chloe's ordinary flat? | «Your flat's lush, I love your sofa» | «You said your dad was a millionaire!» | «This isn't a mansion at all, you lied!» | Nothing, she just went home quietly after tea
exercise: What happened to Chloe's friendship with Mia? | They stayed friends | They never spoke again | Mia told the whole class | Chloe changed school
exercise: Why didn't Oliver's level-100 story add up? | That level takes weeks, not one weekend | The game has only ten levels in it altogether | He doesn't own a computer or any kind of console | He was away at his gran's all that weekend
exercise: What proof did Zara show of meeting the footballer? | A blurry photo of a tall man's back | A signed football shirt in a frame | A video of them talking | A ticket from the airport
exercise: What happened every time Josh's mates asked for his backflip? | He had another excuse | He did a perfect backflip | He did three backflips | He asked them to do one first
exercise: What did Josh's mates ask him to do instead? | A cartwheel, which he could do | A handstand on top of the swings | Run round the park twice | Climb a tree
exercise: Why was Ava's dentist lie found out? | Photos from the other party went on the group chat | The dentist's receptionist rang her friend's house | Her mum told her friend's mum | She forgot the dentist's name
exercise: Where were the friend's five hundred rare cards «kept»? | In a safe at the bank | At his gran's house in the loft | In his school bag, in a tin | On his computer at home
exercise: What did Jaylen's brother really play at college? | Chess | Basketball in the NBA | Football | Tennis
exercise: What place did Lucy really get in the dance competition? | Fifth | First | Last | Second
exercise: How did the class react to Lucy's real result? | They clapped for fifth anyway | They laughed at her | They said fifth is just losing | They asked to see her medal
exercise: Why did Mason never take out his «iPhone 18»? | He said Mum says no phones at school | It was broken and away at the shop being fixed | It was charging at home | He lent it to his cousin
exercise: What does «goated» mean? | The greatest of all time | Very slow and stubborn | Like a farm animal | Bored and tired
exercise: Why do many kids tell boasting lies? | They feel small and want to look cool | They enjoy getting caught out by their friends | Teachers tell them to | They forget what really happened
test: Ryan: «my dad works for Sony innit, secret PS6». What does «innit» add? | It's like saying «right?» | It means «not true» | It means «at home» | It means «tomorrow»
test: Mate: «I've got loads of rare cards but they're in a safe at the bank». What's the sensible view? | It's probably a story, since you can never see them | It must be true, because only rare things go in bank safes | Ask him for the bank's address and go there yourself to look | Get your own safe at the bank to keep your rare cards in
test: Friend: «I'm literally goated at this game». What does he mean? | He's the best ever at it | He's not very good | He plays on a farm | He's quitting the game
test: Zara's photo is a blurry back of a tall man. What would happen if you demanded better proof every time? | You might spoil a fun story — you can just not decide | You'd find out it really was Messi after all | She would show a clear video | Nothing, a photo is always proof
test: Chloe says her dad's a millionaire. You see their small flat. What's the kind move? | Say something nice and let the story drop | Tell the whole class she lied | Laugh at the flat | Ask her how much rent her family pays for it
test: Ava doesn't want to go to her friend's party because she's going to another one. What hurts least? | «Sorry, I'm going to Lily's, I can't do both» | «I've got the dentist» | Just not turning up and saying nothing after | Going to both parties and leaving each one early
test: Lucy: «I won the dance comp!» — she came fifth. Why is the truth braver? | Admitting a real result takes more courage than a fake first | Fifth is really the same as first in dance competitions | Nobody ever checks results | Fake wins feel just as good as real ones
test: Josh boasts he can backflip but can't. What's a good way down from a boast? | «I can't actually, but I can do a cartwheel» | Keep making excuses for months until they forget | Try a backflip without practice | Stop going to the park
test: Mason never shows the phone, and one kid keeps teasing him. Another says «leave it». Who did the kinder thing? | The one who said «leave it» | The one who kept teasing | Both did the same | Neither, they should check his bag
test: Jaylen: «my bro's in the NBA on a secret team». The brother laughs: «I'm on the chess team». What's the lesson? | The real thing about people you love is enough | Chess is a secret NBA team | Brothers always lie about sport | Never talk about your family at school
test: Lad: «level 100 in one weekend, bruv, easy». It takes weeks. What helps you spot this? | Knowing a real fact — how long it takes | Asking him to swear on his mum's life | Believing it if he sounds confident | Checking his shoes
exam: Identify the indication most suggestive that a claim to possess an article is fabricated. | That a reason is invariably given why the article cannot be seen | That the article is described in considerable detail by the claimant | That the claimant is a close friend | That the article is expensive
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the discovery of a boastful falsehood told by a friend who feels inferior. | A kind response may preserve the friendship | Public exposure before the class is invariably required | The friendship must be terminated | The falsehood must be repeated to others
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a false excuse given to avoid a friend's celebration in favour of another? | It occasions greater hurt upon discovery than a kind and honest explanation | It occasions no hurt whatever, the friend being unaware of the other celebration | It is preferable in every case to the truth | It is invariably undiscovered
exam: It is given that a pupil placed fifth in a competition but reported a first place. Determine the statement most consistent with the established facts. | The genuine result was creditable, and its honest report the braver course | The genuine result was wholly without merit, fifth place being a defeat like any other | The false report increased the pupil's credit | No person could verify the result
